Description: Serve the challenge to clients that do not accept gzip
 RenderIndex refused one challenged request in 64, at random, when the
 client did not list gzip in Accept-Encoding, with HTTP 500 and the page
 "client_error_browser". A client without Accept-Encoding accepts any
 coding (RFC 9110, section 12.5.3), and real clients send none: curl by
 default, some accessibility and archiving tools, proxies that strip the
 header. They got a server error at random while the proof of work, the
 actual defence, was never reached. Keel Web saw it on its own test
 client (Keel-Linux/keel-web#2).
 .
 Upstream history: the sampled refusal came with #501 ("ensure that
 clients store cookies", https://github.com/TecharoHQ/anubis/pull/501);
 #1958 (https://github.com/TecharoHQ/anubis/pull/1958, merged after
 1.27.0) changed its answer from 500 to the policy's DENY status and
 logged it at INFO, and left the sampling itself open: its commit says
 "whether the rejection should be sampled needs its own discussion".
 Keel drops it rather than take #1958: a refusal at DENY status is still
 an error page instead of the challenge for a legitimate client, and the
 heuristic stops no scraper that sends "Accept-Encoding: gzip", which
 costs nothing. The proof of work is the real defence, and every client
 without gzip still has to solve it. Not forwarded yet: it belongs in the
 sampling discussion that #1958 leaves open, where Keel will raise it.
 .
 This patch drops the sampled refusal, so such a client gets the
 challenge every time, and adds a test that 4096 requests without gzip,
 with identity and with br are all challenged. The test
 TestChallengeFor_ErrNotFound, skipped as flaky by debian/rules because of
 this refusal, runs again.
